<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaHodaah [The Blessing of Thanksgiving] — Halachah 6<br>Part 6 (§§ 62–75 / Oaseyos סב–עה)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE · Based on <span class="lr">LM I:24</span> (<i>Amtza'isa D'Alma</i>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 62 — <i>SheHechiyanu</i> [Who Has Kept Us Alive]: Time Derives from the Obstructer; Joy Gives Life to Time</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">SheHechiyanu v'kiy'manu v'higi'anu laz'man hazeh</span> [Who has kept us alive, sustained us, and brought us to this time] — blessed over every joy that comes from time to time. The essential <span class="h">Birchas SheHechiyanu</span> [the Blessing of Who Has Kept Us Alive] is on the <span class="h">Yamim Tovim</span>. The essential formation of time derives at its root from the aspect of the waiting until it settles, as above. In truth there is no time at all (as explained on <span class="v">"I have this day begotten you,"</span> <span class="lr">LM II:61</span>). All of time exists only for the sake of free will — man must pass through this world for the span of his allotted days in order to bind time to that which is above time. This world is in place and time; man must pass through it to bind the aspects of time and place to that which is above time (see Hilchos Milah 4 on the above <span class="h">Torah</span>).</div>

<div class="p">The essential time derives from the above waiting — the Arranger and Settler of the mind (= the obstructer). The essential tikun is through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, through <span class="h">mi yaimar</span>. Man comes to this world only for this: to know Hashem through the perception of the above nine chambers, through which the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> light is perceived in <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span>. All this is impossible except through the Arranger and Settler — the aspect of waiting until it settles. All the days of man's life are only in the aspect of waiting — so he may settle properly in true <span class="h">yishuv hada'as</span>, through which he will perceive what he perceives according to his true service — what even the angels do not merit perceiving. For the angels cannot perceive His <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> light in this manner like man can, because they did not properly rectify the Arranger and Settler — which is only rectified through the power of free will that only man possesses (see Hilchos Nefilas Apayim on the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Amtza'isa</span>"). They burn and clamor to perceive Him, and though their perception is very lofty, it has no comparison to the perception of the righteous man who was in this world and withstood his trials through free will — thereby meriting rectifying the mind. The essential tikun is rectifying the Arranger and Settler (= the waiting), from which all time derives. All of man's time — all the days of his life — are the aspect of waiting: he is told to wait the time of his life, to settle himself each day and each moment. Through this he will merit perceiving at each moment what he perceives, until on his final day he ascends with all the days and time he spent in this world — ascending to where he ascends, perceiving the lights of the <span class="h">tzachtzachos</span> that the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> merit: <span class="v">"He shall satisfy your soul with <i>tzachtzachos</i>"</span> — fortunate are they.</div>

<div class="p">The essential thing is merited through performing <span class="h">mitzvos</span> with joy. Joy is the vitality and sustenance of all man's days — for in truth there is no time at all. As we see even physically: they are nothing, for time flies exceedingly — a passing shadow, drifting dust, a fading cloud, etc. The past is gone, the future not yet here, and the present is but an eye-blink (as brought in many <span class="h">sefarim</span>). <span class="mx">"The days of our lives are vanity, vanity of vanities"</span> (Midrash Koheles, Parshah 1) — like the vapor of the seventh vessel, having no substance. Nothing remains of all past, present, and future time except what one snatches from it: some good — Torah, <span class="h">tefilah</span>, <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, good deeds, intense desire and longing for Hashem, closeness to <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and true friends, etc. The essential thing is joy — the point of all <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, the essential vitality and sustenance.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential vitality (in time and place) is through the day and time one merits the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> — rejoicing in Him: <span class="v">"this is the day Hashem has made — let us exult and rejoice in it."</span> Only <span class="v">"this is the day Hashem has made"</span> — when one merits exulting and rejoicing in it. From there comes all vitality and sustenance of the days and times (= the days of man's life). The wicked, who do not merit the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, truly have no vitality — therefore our Sages say: <span class="mx">"the wicked in their lifetimes are called dead,"</span> since they do not merit binding time to that which is above time, receiving vitality from the Source of Life through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>. Conversely, even the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, if they do not take care to wait until they settle properly and their minds pursue excessively — they cannot live in this world at all: <span class="v">"when they drew near before Hashem and died"</span>; <span class="mx">"he peeked and died."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential vitality of the days and times man lives in this world is through the above — meriting the tikun of the Settler and Arranger properly through performing <span class="h">mitzvos</span> with joy. Then he merits coming and ascending with all his days and times to where he ascends: <span class="v">"Avraham was old, coming with his days"</span> — <span class="mx">"he was old and entered with his days,"</span> as the holy Zohar states. Therefore at every time of joy we bless: <span class="h">SheHechiyanu v'kiy'manu v'higi'anu laz'man hazeh</span> [Who has kept us alive, sustained us, and brought us to this time] — the essential vitality and sustenance of all our days is through reaching this time of joy: <span class="v">"this is the day Hashem has made."</span> These are the sanctified times of the <span class="h">Yamim Tovim</span>, where His G-dliness is revealed through the great miracles and wonders He performed (= the essential joy), or when one sees a friend and rejoices (= the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 63 — <i>SheHechiyanu</i> [Who Has Kept Us Alive] on a New Fruit: All Growth Derives from the Joy of <i>Mitzvos</i></div>

<div class="p">Even on a new fruit we bless <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> [Who Has Kept Us Alive], as our Sages say: <span class="mx">"I also say the blessing of <i>z'man</i> on a new pumpkin."</span> All the growing and sprouting of fruits is through the joy of <span class="h">mitzvos</span> that sustains the entire world (created for the Torah's sake). As explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Arichas Apayim</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:155</span>): all the power of growth and sprouting is through <span class="h">emunah</span> (= joy). As it says: <span class="v">"the meadows are clothed with flocks, the valleys covered with grain — they shout for joy, they also sing."</span> And in many verses the great joy through the sprouting of fruits is explained: <span class="v">"the blossoms have appeared in the land, the time of singing has arrived."</span> All the growing and sprouting of fruits each year is through the point of <span class="h">mitzvos</span> (= joy in Him). Fruits and grain sustain every living soul — essentially man, whose essential vitality is the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> to know Him — through performing <span class="h">mitzvos</span> with joy. Therefore we bless <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> [Who Has Kept Us Alive] over them.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 64 — The Thirty-Day Interval: Repairing the Moon's Blemish</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential <span class="h">birchas haz'man</span> [blessing of time] requires seeing the thing after at least thirty days — for continuous pleasure is no pleasure (as is known). The essential vitality is through the above waiting — the root of joy. The measure is thirty days (= a month), corresponding to the filling of the moon's blemish. Then is the essential tikun of joy — repairing <span class="v">"luminaries"</span> (deficient), from which all sadness derives (as in ois lamed above) — transforming it to joy.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 65 — Past, Present, and Future: The Threefold Holiness of Time Through Joy</div>

<div class="p">One must revive and gladden his soul through Hashem's kindnesses and goodness — what He did for us in the past, what He does in the present, and what He will do in the future: <span class="mx">"He was good, He is good, He will be good to us."</span> This is all the vitality and sustenance of time (= the days of man's life), whose essential thing is to bind them to that which is above time. Each person must search and seek to find within himself good points from the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> he merited performing each day from his youth. For we already merited receiving the Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> through Moshe Rabbainu, which are our life and the length of our days (as explained extensively, especially on <span class="v">"I shall sing to my G-d while I yet live"</span>). Even now, however things are, he snatches some <span class="h">mitzvos</span> every single day — <span class="mx">"even the sinners of Israel are full of <i>mitzvos</i> as a pomegranate."</span> The essential thing: to rejoice in this and know the truth — there is no vitality in the world besides this.</div>

<div class="p">The essential thing is what we hope for and anticipate — a good end, through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, as all the prophets and true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (first and last) have strengthened us. <span class="mx">"All Israel has a portion in the World to Come."</span> Even if he is as he is — as long as he believes there is no vitality except Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, strives and yearns for the eternal purpose, draws close to true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and friends, and strengthens himself to gladden his soul with the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>-points — he will certainly merit a good end.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore holiness is tripled in all aspects: <span class="v">"Holy, holy, holy"</span>; <span class="mx">"the threefold light in threefold time."</span> The essential holiness is joy — through three aspects that are the totality of time: past, present, and future. Their entire vitality and holiness is the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> — through which time is bound to that which is above time, the very purpose for which man comes to the world.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 66 — Shlomo HaMelech in Koheles: The Joy of the <i>Mitzvah</i> Is the Only True Good</div>

<div class="p">This is why Shlomo HaMelech warned greatly in his book Koheles about the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>: <span class="v">"there is nothing better for a man than to eat and drink and be glad"</span> — our Sages explain: eating and drinking of Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, and rejoicing on <span class="h">Shabbosos</span> and <span class="h">Yamim Tovim</span>. This is the essential thing. Shlomo speaks at great length in Koheles about the world's vanity, repeating many times the great futility — so man does not deceive himself. Therefore he says: the essential tikun is the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> — <span class="v">"there is nothing better for man,"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">He also says: <span class="v">"also every man to whom G-d has given... and to rejoice in his labor"</span> (Koheles 5). <span class="v">"For he shall not much remember the days of his life, because G-d answers him with the joy of his heart"</span> — Rashi explains: Hashem testifies about the joy of his heart — that he rejoiced to do good. This adjoins <span class="v">"he shall not much remember the days of his life"</span>: the verse strongly urges man to strengthen himself in joy all the days of his life. Since his days are few and fly exceedingly (<span class="v">"not much"</span>) and Koheles speaks extensively of the world's vanity — therefore: <span class="v">"he shall not much"</span> — his days are very few. Let him remember the days of his life — meaning: let him remember to vivify them with true life and sustenance worthy of being called "the days of his life." This is through holy joy — all the vitality. <span class="v">"G-d answers him with the joy of his heart"</span> — Hashem testifies about his heart's joy that he rejoiced to do good. He settled his mind in this world and knew the truth: there is no true vitality except Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> — <span class="v">"for they are our life"</span> — therefore his heart was glad to do good. This is the entire purpose and true sustenance of all the days of his life.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 67 — Settling the Mind: The Counsel of Joy Against All Sadness</div>

<div class="p">The principle: man must settle his mind very well at all times — what is he doing in this passing world? What will be his end? What is the purpose of all worldly pursuits and desires? (as explained in <span class="lr">LM II:10</span>). The essential obstacle is sadness — every person is full of sadness, worries, sorrow, and sighing: from livelihood, from the great pain of children, etc. — especially from the multitude of sins: "woe is me from my <span class="h">yaitzer</span>; woe is me from my Maker." Although the <span class="h">mussar</span> books say that sadness over sins is good — the <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> and the Chambers of Exchange overpower through such sadness until one is distanced from Hashem even more. The worry over sins would be good if one returned in <span class="h">teshuvah</span> through it — casting away all worldly pursuits and returning completely to Hashem. But the majority of people, especially in these generations, are distanced even more through excessive sadness (even over sins). The Chambers of Exchange reverse the truth and confuse the mind, implanting, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, despair in the heart — as if there is no more hope to renew his days like the eagle. They strengthen the sadness of livelihood (<span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it"</span>) — the craving for wealth, to conduct one's household extravagantly with fine dwellings, household vessels, silver and gold and jewelry — all far beyond one's means. Whatever Hashem provides is never enough: <span class="mx">"the more possessions, the more worry."</span> Especially those whose livelihood is pressed, etc. There are many differences among people, but most are filled with sadness, worries, and sorrows without limit — and this is the greatest damage, for through it one cannot settle his mind as to what he is doing in this world.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must strengthen himself exceedingly to gladden his soul through all the counsel and ways Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> revealed (as explained in the holy <span class="h">sefarim</span> and in our writings extensively). One must remind himself at all times of all the kindnesses, goodnesses, and wonders Hashem has already done for us — collectively and individually: <span class="v">"bless Hashem, my soul, and do not forget all His bounties."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of remembering the Exodus from Egypt every day: <span class="v">"so that you remember the day you left Egypt all the days of your life"</span>; <span class="v">"remember this day when you left Egypt."</span> For this all the holy festivals were established — to rejoice in them: <span class="v">"you shall rejoice on your festival"</span> — all remembering the Exodus. One must take to heart and remind himself well of all the kindnesses and wonders of the Exodus and the giving of the Torah, through which we have eternal hope (each and every one of us). <span class="mx">"In every generation, a person must see himself as if he himself left Egypt"</span> — for certainly it was not for nothing that He bestowed such kindness with such miracles and wonders, taking us out of Egypt, giving us the Torah, and drawing us close to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in every generation. This kindness will certainly endure forever — <span class="v">"the word of our G-d shall stand forever."</span> And even now He performs miracles daily — what we merit snatching each day: many <span class="h">mitzvos</span> — <span class="h">tzitzis</span>, <span class="h">tefillin</span>, <span class="h">Krias Shma</span>, <span class="h">tefilah</span>, Torah study, etc. We trust, wait, and anticipate salvation at all times — in the end it will surely come, it will not delay: <span class="v">"there is hope for your future."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 68 — "Why Do You Leap, O Mountains?": The Foundation of Holiness Can Never Be Destroyed</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Why do you leap, you hunchbacked mountains? The mountain G-d desired for His dwelling — Hashem shall dwell there forever."</span> The <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> — the idolaters and wicked who oppose holiness — arise each time to uproot everything, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, wanting to tear down to the very foundation: <span class="v">"remember, Hashem, against the children of Edom the day of Yerushalayim — who said: raze it, raze it, to its very foundation!"</span>; <span class="v">"against Your people they scheme, and conspire against Your sheltered ones; they say: come, let us destroy them as a nation, and the name of Israel shall be remembered no more."</span> This is true for every person at all times: <span class="v">"the wicked watches the righteous and seeks to kill him"</span>; <span class="mx">"every day a man's evil inclination renews against him and seeks to kill him"</span> — <span class="v">"but Hashem will not abandon him to his hand."</span></div>

<div class="p">For we already have a strong foundation — we are the children of Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov; we already received the holy Torah; we are strong in our holy <span class="h">emunah</span> and boast in it: <span class="mx">"fortunate are we — how good is our portion!"</span> — arising and retiring, unifying His Name twice daily. Therefore every Jew must revive himself through the foundation of <span class="h">emunah</span> within him — the essential foundation upon which everything stands: <span class="mx">"Chavakuk came and established them on one — the holy <i>emunah</i>"</span>: <span class="v">"the righteous shall live by his <i>emunah</i>."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must remember the Exodus daily — we then saw His G-dliness revealed, eye to eye, through all the awesome signs and wonders: <span class="v">"remember His wonders that He performed."</span> In every generation, great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> come and establish foundations that can never be demolished: <span class="v">"a <i>tzadik</i> is the foundation of the world."</span> This is the foundation of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, for which David labored his entire life until he found its Place and foundation: <span class="v">"remember, Hashem, for David... who swore to Hashem... if I give sleep to my eyes... until I find a place for Hashem."</span> The essential thing is the foundation of holiness — the Place and foundation of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> that David found (= <span class="h">Mashiach</span>). Upon this foundation the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was built twice. Though through our many sins the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> overpowered and destroyed the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> twice — their effort was in vain, for the foundation can never be destroyed: <span class="v">"this is My resting place forever; here I shall dwell, for I have desired it."</span></div>

<div class="p">Though it is certainly a great, bitter suffering that the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was destroyed (and one must mourn and lament at midnight every night), one cannot lament all day — one must console oneself, as established in <span class="h">Tikun Chatzos</span> and the <span class="h">kinos</span>, concluding with words of comfort. Through sadness and lament alone, life is impossible — the essential vitality is joy.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, one who wishes to work on his eternal end must cry out each day (especially at midnight) over his sins that delay the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>'s rebuilding — the essential crying being over the obstruction and prevention of joy: <span class="v">"the joy of our hearts has ceased."</span> One must cry and pray until Hashem has mercy, consoling us from our hearts' sadness, gladdening us with His salvation — through what we already merited: receiving the Torah, drawing close to true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, all the points of Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> we already merited through His kindness and great wonders. The essential thing: the foundation of <span class="h">emunah</span> (= the foundation of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> that David found = <span class="h">Mashiach</span>) — this can never be destroyed.</div>

<div class="p">They destroyed only the building above the foundation — the aspect of the roof [<span class="h">gag</span>]. The entire building relative to the foundation is the aspect of the roof. <span class="v">"What ails you that you went up entirely to the rooftops?"</span> — the verse says: what do you boast about? — <span class="v">"you went up entirely to the rooftops"</span>: the entire destruction is only the rooftops — the building above the foundation. But the building's foundation (= the essential building) can never be destroyed.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Why do you leap, you hunchbacked mountains?"</span> — <span class="h">gavnunim</span> [hunchbacked] = <span class="h">gav</span> [back] = roof and upper story. David tells the destroyers: why do you leap and dance, you hunchbacked mountains? All your might is only over the <span class="h">gavnunim</span> — you destroyed the back and roof (= the building above the foundation). But the very foundation of holiness — the foundation of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — you can never destroy: <span class="v">"the mountain G-d desired for His dwelling — Hashem shall dwell there forever."</span> The foundation of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> that David <span class="h">Mashiach</span> found — this will remain forever, until it is rebuilt speedily in our days as an eternal structure never to be destroyed.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"when you build a new house"</span> — alluding to the Third <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, when a new light will shine upon Tziyon: <span class="v">"behold, I am doing a new thing; now it shall spring forth."</span> The Third <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> is therefore called a "new house." <span class="v">"You shall make a railing for your roof, that the one who falls shall not fall from it"</span> — each person must now engage in building the future new house. The essential thing: to make a railing for the roof so the building above the foundation is built and fortified well, never to be destroyed again, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. <span class="v">"You shall not place blood in your house, for the one who falls shall fall"</span> — all transgressions are the aspect of "blood" (coming through the intensifying of blood in the 365 sinews corresponding to the 365 negative commandments). Through this the fallen have fallen, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>, and through this the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was destroyed. Therefore one must repair the building: <span class="v">"make a railing for your roof"</span> — building the new house with such tikunim that no more "blood" will be placed in it, no more sinning, no more falling — everyone strengthening themselves to stand firm in their <span class="h">emunah</span>, so the House will endure forever.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 69 — The Length of Exile: Hashem Awaits an Eternal Building</div>

<div class="p">This is why the exile is so long — Hashem awaits an eternal building, a complete <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> with no exile after it. This requires the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of each generation to make such tikunim that Israel will never again fall from their holiness, never relapse: <span class="v">"I shall remove the heart of stone from your flesh... I shall cause you to walk in My statutes."</span> Therefore it is impossible to fathom the wondrous end — for one with free will it is extremely difficult to help. Yet <span class="v">"Hashem's counsel shall stand forever."</span> In His mercy He consoles and gladdens us through the holy foundations we already merited — the Exodus, the Torah, through which every Jew snatches many <span class="h">mitzvos</span> daily. David already found the place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>; it was already built twice; they offered <span class="h">ketores</span> and sacrifices; they clarified all the <span class="h">halachos</span> of the Torah in the <span class="h">Lishkas HaGazis</span>. Through all this, the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> established strong foundations for us forever — even in our destruction, these holy foundations cannot be demolished.</div>

<div class="p">After the destruction, our G-d did not abandon us. Awesome, great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> arose in every generation — for no generation is an orphan — even now in our generations there were awesome <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who established foundations that can never be demolished: <span class="v">"though I have scattered them among the nations and dispersed them through the lands, I shall be for them a miniature <i>Mikdash</i>"</span> — <span class="mx">"these are the synagogues and study halls."</span> Through this we anticipate and await the building of the new House that will never be destroyed.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore every person must gladden himself each day through the miracles and salvations of past, present, and future — the foundation of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, established since the days of Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov who prayed there (as our Sages say). Moshe Rabbainu stressed this Place many times: <span class="v">"the place Hashem shall choose."</span> David <span class="h">Mashiach</span> merited finding it through immense toil. This is the joy of the past miracles and salvations. The present miracles and salvations: the synagogues and study halls where Torah and <span class="h">tefilah</span> are engaged in daily (= a miniature <span class="h">Mikdash</span>). The essential joy: through the hope we hold for the Third <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> in the future, which will endure forever — when joy will increase beyond limit: <span class="v">"then our mouths will be filled with laughter."</span></div>

<div class="p">So too each person must gladden his soul through all the good points he has already merited — especially that he was not made a gentile and was separated from those who err, etc. — and what he still merits snatching each day. And through the hope for the future: ultimately every Jew will certainly be rectified — <span class="v">"Hashem will not abandon His people"</span>; <span class="v">"Hashem did not speak of wiping out the name of Israel"</span> (said even of generations that served idolatry, as explained in Melachim II). All through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in whose shelter we take refuge — through whom everything will be rectified. They are all our joy, vitality, and sustenance forever.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 70 — Three Redemptions, Three Transformations, Three Peaces: Past, Present, and Future</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the three <span class="h">p'duyos</span> [redemptions], three <span class="h">hafuchos</span> [transformations], and three <span class="h">sh'lomos</span> [peaces]. They are the aspect of redemption and sweetening of judgments — nullifying sadness (= the severity of judgment, from which all exiles of body and soul, and all suffering, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>) — drawing joy and peace (= <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>, freedom, and the nullification of all suffering). Therefore they are recited on <span class="h">Motzaay Shabbos</span> — so sadness and judgments (which awaken during the weekdays) do not overpower. For this reason we recite <span class="h">Vayitain Lecha</span> etc. — many verses of joy, blessing, and peace — to draw the joy of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> into the weekdays, nullifying the sadness that wants to take hold during the week. The three sets of three correspond to the three aspects of past, present, and future — through which one must gladden oneself, as above.</div>

<div class="p">The three <span class="h">p'duyos</span> begin: <span class="v">"He redeemed my soul in peace"</span> — past tense: He already redeemed us by choosing us from all nations, giving us His Torah, separating us from those who err — from distress to relief countless times. The second: <span class="v">"the people said to Shaul: 'shall Yonasan die, who wrought this great salvation in Israel?'... the people redeemed Yonasan and he did not die"</span> — alluding to every person's dangers and accusations, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>. Hashem in His mercy sends into the hearts of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> to defend him — the kindnesses and goodnesses of the present: what Hashem bestows upon the guilty through the great power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who sustain the world and defend all. The third: <span class="v">"the redeemed of Hashem shall return and come to Tziyon with song; eternal joy upon their heads... gladness and joy shall they attain, and sorrow and sighing shall flee"</span> — the essential hope and joy of the future, when we return to Tziyon and merit eternal joy. The essential thing: to transform all sorrow and sighing to joy.</div>

<div class="p">The three <span class="h">hafuchos</span>: First — <span class="v">"You have transformed my mourning to dance; You opened my sackcloth and girded me with joy"</span> — all past tense: the past kindnesses — He performed miracles without number, transforming mourning to dance, from distress to relief countless times. Second — <span class="v">"Hashem your G-d did not wish to listen to Bil'am, and Hashem your G-d transformed the curse to a blessing"</span> — the miracles and salvations in the present: awesome accusations arise each time from the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> of wicked Bil'am who wants to uproot Israel, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Hashem in His great mercy refuses to listen and transforms the curse to blessing — through the great power of Moshe Rabbainu (= the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of each generation). Third — <span class="v">"then the maiden shall rejoice in dance... I shall transform their mourning to joy"</span> — the joy of the future.</div>

<div class="p">The three <span class="h">sh'lomos</span>: First — <span class="v">"[He] creates the speech of the lips: peace, peace, to the far and near, says Hashem, and I have healed him"</span> — past tense: He already prepared healing (= peace) for the far and near through the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who prepared for us elixirs of life and precious, holy remedies in their holy books and words, which restore the soul through comforting counsel. <span class="v">"Taste and see that Hashem is good."</span> Second — <span class="v">"a spirit clothed Amasai... 'peace to you, and peace to your helpers'"</span> — alluding to the war against the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in every generation (as is well known from what occurs in our generations and earlier). Hashem in His mercy sends into the hearts of many Jewish souls to look upon the truth — they come to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and those attached to them and say: <span class="v">"peace to you and peace to your helper."</span> Third — <span class="v">"you shall say: thus to life! And you — peace"</span> — alluding to the eternal life of the future, when the wondrous peace will be as explained in many verses.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Hashem will give strength to His people; Hashem will bless His people with peace."</span> The essential completeness of joy and blessing is peace — the vessel that holds blessing. Through peace — many Jewish souls gathering together — the light of joy increases. Through this one can receive the light of the above nine chambers, perceiving the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> light in <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span>. Due to the great intensity of this light, it can only be received through many vessels — many Jewish souls gathering together in love and peace around the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who merit these lights (the lights of the <span class="h">tzachtzachos</span> that heal and gladden all who are ill in soul, even the very gravely ill). The more gravely ill they are, the more precious the remedies needed — which only the greatest <span class="h">tzadik</span> knows how to draw and heal with (as explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Mishra</span>," <span class="lr">LM I:30</span>; see there). The essential thing is through peace: <span class="v">"for with joy you shall go out and in peace be led."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 71 — The Three Sounds of the <i>Shofar</i>: Past, Present, and Future</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the three sounds of the <span class="h">shofar</span>: essentially <span class="h">tekiah</span>, <span class="h">teruah</span>, <span class="h">tekiah</span> (our Sages had a doubt about the <span class="h">teruah</span> sound, therefore they established blowing both <span class="h">teruah</span> and <span class="h">shevarim</span>, as explained in the Gemara). The <span class="h">tekiah</span> before is a simple sound, and the <span class="h">tekiah</span> after is a simple sound. These represent the good and kindness He already performed for us at the giving of the Torah — given with the sound of the <span class="h">shofar</span>, a simple sound, for then the crookedness of the heart was completely straightened. They merited great freedom: <span class="mx">"freedom from the evil inclination, from the angel of death, and from the subjugation of kingdoms."</span> And in the future: <span class="v">"on that day a great <i>shofar</i> shall be blown, and the lost ones shall come"</span> — a simple sound, for then: <span class="v">"they shall all know Me, from the least to the greatest."</span> All suffering and sadness from the crookedness of the heart will be completely nullified.</div>

<div class="p">In between, we blow the <span class="h">teruah</span> — <span class="h">teruah</span> and <span class="h">shevarim</span>: groaning and wailing, as our Sages say. This is what man must do now, in the present, all the days of his life: overpower to transform all kinds of sadness (= groaning and wailing) to joy. Through this comes the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — through <span class="h">yishuv hada'as</span>, which is through joy. The essential thing: through the power of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">shofar</span>, we merit truly awakening, implanting holy <span class="h">emunah</span> in our hearts (= all our joy): <span class="v">"fortunate is the people that knows the <i>teruah</i>; Hashem, in the light of Your countenance they shall walk"</span> — perceiving the light of the countenance, from which all holy <span class="h">da'as</span> to know that Hashem is G-d: <span class="v">"in Your Name they shall exult all day."</span> Until we merit seizing all the sorrow and sighing (from which all sins come) and transforming it to joy — the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: sins transformed to merits. This is the sound of the <span class="h">teruah</span> — the essential thing.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 72 — The <i>Mizmor</i> "Hodu" Before <i>Tefilah</i>: Past, Present, and Future in Songs</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the entire <span class="h">mizmor</span> <span class="v">"Hodu LaHashem"</span> recited before <span class="h">tefilah</span> at the beginning of <span class="h">Pesukay d'Zimrah</span> — speaking of past, present, and future. <span class="v">"Remember His wonders that He performed"</span>; <span class="v">"remember forever His covenant, the word He commanded for a thousand generations, which He made with Avraham"</span> etc. — He gave us the Torah and brought us into <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, the portion of our inheritance — all speaking of the great past miracles. Then: <span class="v">"sing to Hashem a new song; proclaim from day to day His salvation"</span> — even each and every day He performs wondrous, awesome salvations for us. Then the future: <span class="v">"let the heavens rejoice and the earth exult, let the sea roar and its fullness... then all the trees of the forest shall sing before Hashem"</span> — all said of the future.</div>

<div class="p">In all this we must gladden ourselves at all times — especially before <span class="h">tefilah</span> — and thank His Name for the miracles He already performed, what He does now at all times (expanding for us in distress, etc.), and what He will do for us for eternity through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in whose shelter we take refuge. This entire <span class="h">mizmor</span> "Hodu" was recited by David before the Ark when it entered its place (as explained in Divray HaYamim). The essential joy is when the Ark comes to its place — deriving from <span class="v">"blessed is the glory of Hashem from His place"</span>: the source of joy. The essential joy is the Torah — drawn from there. Likewise in all the songs and praises, most speak of past, present, and future — all to gladden our souls in the depth of our long exile of body and soul, so that we merit returning, drawing close, and clinging to Hashem — essentially through joy.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 73 — <i>Ehyeh Asher Ehyeh</i>: "With the Attribute of Mercy I Created, Conduct, and Will Renew the World"</div>

<div class="p">This is what is found in <span class="h">Osiyos d'Rabbi Akiva</span> (cited in Yalkut Reuveni, <span class="h">Parshas Shemos</span>): <span class="mx">"Another interpretation: <i>Ehyeh Asher Ehyeh</i> — with the attribute of mercy I created the world, with the attribute of mercy I conduct it, and with the attribute of mercy I will renew it. Another interpretation: with the attribute of good I created the world, with the attribute of good I conduct it, and I will renew it in the future."</span> This is the above: <span class="mx">"He was good, He is good, He will be good"</span> — through which comes the essential joy and clinging to Him (= the vitality and sustenance). <span class="h">Ehyeh</span> is <span class="h">Keser</span>, as explained there on <span class="v">"I shall be with you and bless you."</span> <span class="h">Keser</span> (= the Place, the Arranger) is the source of joy — from which comes the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> from Egypt and all exiles. The passage concludes: <span class="mx">"another interpretation: with the attribute of <i>emunah</i> I created the world"</span> — for <span class="h">emunah</span> is the aspect of joy, the foundation of everything.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 74 — The Three Redemptions at <i>Hatavas Chalom</i>: Joy Rectifies Dreams</div>

<div class="p">Therefore these three <span class="h">p'duyos</span> etc. are recited at <span class="h">Hatavas Chalom</span> [the amelioration of a dream]. The essential tikun of a dream is through joy — as the <span class="h">Hatavas Chalom</span> concludes: <span class="v">"go eat your bread with joy and drink your wine with a glad heart."</span> As already explained (ois lamed-ches): sleep derives at its root from the wearying of the mind through the pursuit and obstructer; according to the tikun of the mind, so the sleep and the dreams. Therefore the essential tikun of a bad dream is through drawing the joy of the three comforters — through which the essential tikun of the mind comes. Therefore we recite then the three <span class="h">p'duyos</span>, three <span class="h">hafuchos</span>, etc. — through which joy and peace are drawn: the essential redemption from all suffering, nullified through joy.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 75 — Closing Note: "I Shall Rejoice in Hashem" — The Joy of <i>Shma Yisrael</i></div>

<div class="p">See the holy Zohar, <span class="h">Parshas Pinchas</span> (daf 236b), on the verse <span class="v">"Shma Yisrael"</span> etc. The passage states: <span class="mx">"Rav Hamnuna Saba said: whoever performs this unification each day — joy is prepared for him from above, from the secret of these letters. Shin-Mem from this side, and Alef-Ches from this side, etc. And the sign: <i>Esmach</i> [I shall rejoice], as it is written: '<i>Anochi</i> <i>esmach</i> <i>baHashem</i>' — in Hashem Himself — this is the holy unification, and it is fitting."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is the entire point of all our words in this entire discourse: the inner essence of our words is that every Jew must rejoice in Hashem — <span class="v">"<i>Anochi esmach baHashem</i>"</span> [I shall rejoice in Hashem] — this is the essential <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> and salvation of every Jew. The essential thing: every Jew, however he is, must rejoice in what he merits — at least saying <span class="h">Shma Yisrael</span> twice every day, as we boast: <span class="mx">"fortunate are we — how good is our portion!"</span> — arising and retiring, unifying His Name twice daily. This is all our vitality and hope in this world and the World to Come forever. Nothing will remain of man or of fleeting time except the joy of holy <span class="h">emunah</span>, as explained above. The holy Zohar reveals that the letters of <span class="h">Esmach</span> [I shall rejoice] are hinted in <span class="h">Shin-Mem</span> of <span class="h">Shma</span> and <span class="h">Alef-Ches</span> of <span class="h">Echad</span> — for one must rejoice greatly in meriting to unify His Name each day: <span class="v">"<i>Anochi esmach baHashem</i>."</span></div>

